{
 "id": "tray-arm-lattice-fdm",
 "kind": "lattice",
 "brief": "A cantilever arm that holds a small cable tray off the side of my the robot's base, in PETG on an\nFDM printer. The arm runs along +X from the base's side wall at x = 0 to x = 80; it is 30 mm wide\n(y = -15 to 15) and 40 mm tall (z = 0 to 40), and it prints standing just like that, z up, with its\nbottom face on the bed. At x = 0 a 4 mm plate (x = 0 to 4, the full 30 x 40) bolts to the base with\ntwo M4 screws along X at (y, z) = (0, 10) and (0, 30); their heads are on the arm side, so leave\n8 mm diameter clear for each from x = 4 to x = 12. At the far end a 6 mm plate (x = 74 to 80) takes\ntwo M3 heat-set inserts along X at (y, z) = (-8, 30) and (8, 30), in 4 mm holes from x = 73.5\noutward. The tray pushes 60 N straight down on the inserts, and if someone bumps it, 20 N sideways\n(along Y) with 30 N down. Safety factor 2. Between the plates I want an open strut lattice, not a\nsolid web, under 55 g, and it has to print without any support: my printer bridges about 8 mm\ncleanly and nothing longer.",
